{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point","id":486919,"title":"Vinculum as a Breaking Point","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-20T18:31:51-07:00","id":486919,"title":"Vinculum as a Breaking Point","content":"I always found it odd that making a ghoul (and thus likely soon inflicting a vinculum) was a Humanity 8 sin. I know that the book doesn't say that the vinculum is Humanity 8 (can temporarily ghoul someone to save their life with only a one step blood bond) and I can pretty readily understand *why* Onyx Path didn't weigh in with a sin placement for a vinculum as it's a whole big can of worms. I'm just curious as to where other people would place it on their chart.<br /> <br /> Personally, I see vinculums as deeply corruptive things. They immediately place a wedge between a human and everyone around them as everything is shuffled down a rank. Their efforts to please their master is likely to ruin their marriage, leave their kids in trouble, etc. as they're hardly likely to (often) plead off seeing their regnant and doing tasks for them in order to go to their kid's concert and may get so wrapped up in the task that they forget to even pick their kids up from the After School Care. Naturally they'd never bother their regnant with such things, because if their regnant does care, it'll make them feel bad, and who'd want that?<br /> <br /> Besides all of that, it's also a deeply vampiric act that utterly changes a mortal - vampire's relationship in a way that utterly subverts the person's own individual instincts, beliefs and desires, in terms of the vampire, and causes the rest of their world to realign. This realignment may be in an utterly unexpected and hard to control direction, but it's there. The vampire basically gets to experience how an intrinsic part of their nature, their own blood, can turn someone else into a devoted slave, and that's got to be at least the same level as suffering massive damage that would hospitalise a human, if not lower.<br /> <br /> With a well-picked individual, especially with consent, it could be that little bit higher up the chart, but in my estimation, going to that honest cop and corrupting him to your goals absolutely (thus destroying everything he cherished about himself) or your friend who was starting to freak out and considered leaving (betraying them in an eternal trap) would be lower because there's something intrinsically vampiric, again, about negating another person's will permanently for your own desires.<br /> <br /> Especially as the blood is also addictive and so it's not like they can walk away from it just fine if you Forgetful Mind their memories away or die and leave them without a wrist to tap.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the new devoted slave will attempt to convince the vampire that this was the best thing that happened to them, even if they don't quite believe it themselves, which is something you won't get from murdering a person.<br /> <br /> So yes, do you have it as a Breaking Point below 8? If not, why not? (I can see an argument that the slave's devotion is so absolute it can trick the vampire). If so, why so?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My horror roleplaying blog with a strong focus on World of Darkness and Call of Cthulhu can be found here: <a href=\"http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/\" target=\"_blank\">http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1248-laraqua","name":"Laraqua","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"107"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T19:08:16-07:00","id":486961,"title":"","content":"I think it's a reasonable Breaking Poimt, since it's pretty distinctly inhuman thing to do. I would put it at a higher level than making a ghoul, though. Probably Humanity 9?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T19:41:16-07:00","id":486968,"title":"","content":"That's cool. I'd personally put it at Humanity 5, since it's (personally) one of the most terrifying things that could happen to me (in my own mind). <br /> <br /> Depending on circumstances, I'd rather die!<br /> <br /> But I can certainly see a Humanity 9. Less impact on game styles at the very least.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My horror roleplaying blog with a strong focus on World of Darkness and Call of Cthulhu can be found here: <a href=\"http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/\" target=\"_blank\">http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1248-laraqua","name":"Laraqua","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"107"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T19:48:42-07:00","id":486971,"title":"","content":"9 sounds right. It's not really about how horrifying it is, but how inhuman it is. Ghouling is at 8 because it turns an ordinary mortal into a potentially immortal being with vampire powers that can sustain their existence on the blood of vampires, a pretty inhuman concept. However objectionable a thing a vinculum is, it's an idea that's been around in humanity's mind for a long time, and you can certainly have similarly twisted relationships without the benefit of magic vampire blood. So it makes sense that it would be higher than ghouling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T20:12:17-07:00","id":486977,"title":"","content":"Ah, I see your point. Mine is more that in terms of getting comfortable with an idea though my gut instinct is more personal. As you get closer to the Beast, you see humans more like toys and playthings, and inflicting vinculums on a regular person plays into that perspective. Humans are food. Humans are toys. Humans are there for the vampire's enjoyment. Witnessing the innate effects of one's own blood enforce such utter servitude and addiction, would be a major reminder (in my mind) of what a vampire is and their relationship to humanity. At least a magical power is somewhat divorced from your own physicality. Blood bonds are also such a quintessentially vampiric thing, as nothing else has such an easy and automatic override into someone else's mind that lasts for so long.<br /> <br /> But I like your reasoning so I'd bump it up in my own personal game rules at Humanity 6, putting it on par with walking out of a severe car wreck. Also because the idea of a Humanity 5 vampire caring one whit about putting a vinculum on someone doesn't seem right to me. I could see an argument for having it at Humanity 7, because that would put it on par with the changes in interactions and relationships that you get from living in a covenant, but I personally see it as a bigger part of being a Vampire then that. More like being able to walk away with your guts half hanging out and no worries. A *lot* of problems disappear with a vinculum (and new ones appear, but hey, that's part of the fun).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My horror roleplaying blog with a strong focus on World of Darkness and Call of Cthulhu can be found here: <a href=\"http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/\" target=\"_blank\">http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1248-laraqua","name":"Laraqua","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"107"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T20:48:51-07:00","id":486989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laraqua</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point?p=486977#post486977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, I see your point. Mine is more that in terms of getting comfortable with an idea though my gut instinct is more personal. As you get closer to the Beast, you see humans more like toys and playthings, and inflicting vinculums on a regular person plays into that perspective. Humans are food. Humans are toys. Humans are there for the vampire's enjoyment. Witnessing the innate effects of one's own blood enforce such utter servitude and addiction, would be a major reminder (in my mind) of what a vampire is and their relationship to humanity. At least a magical power is somewhat divorced from your own physicality. Blood bonds are also such a quintessentially vampiric thing, as nothing else has such an easy and automatic override into someone else's mind that lasts for so long.<br /> <br /> But I like your reasoning so I'd bump it up in my own personal game rules at Humanity 6, putting it on par with walking out of a severe car wreck. Also because the idea of a Humanity 5 vampire caring one whit about putting a vinculum on someone doesn't seem right to me. I could see an argument for having it at Humanity 7, because that would put it on par with the changes in interactions and relationships that you get from living in a covenant, but I personally see it as a bigger part of being a Vampire then that. More like being able to walk away with your guts half hanging out and no worries. A *lot* of problems disappear with a vinculum (and new ones appear, but hey, that's part of the fun).</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, if you're used to objectifying humans, thinking of them as toys to be played with, then vinculum would seem perfectly natural. &quot;<i>Of course</i> I can innately make them subservient to me, that's what they're <i>there for</i>&quot;.<br /> <br /> It doesn't really matter that it's horrifying to you as a human, to a vampire it's likely going to become second nature very quickly. One of the first things you learn as a vampire is to dehumanize the living. You have to in order to survive, because they're your food. So right away, you're naturally going to get very comfortable with the idea that you can make them <i>act</i> like the sheep they are with just a small sip of your Vitae. As an everyday part of a vampire's unlife, Vinculum should definitely be a very high Humanity Breaking Point, because only the vampires who try <i>very</i> hard to be human are going to be as horrified by it as you are.<br /> <br /> Remember, in 2e, Humanity Breaking Points are not sins against an objective moral standard. They're just things that are jarring to a vampire and remind them &quot;oh yeah, I'm a fucking monster.&quot; The fact that it's horrifying to you indicates that it would be a harsh <i>Integrity</i> Breaking Point for <i>you</i> to witness or experience. The fact that the vampire is so nonplussed by this atrocious act probably makes it seem all the worse to you, but it's actually an indication that it's a very <i>high</i> level <i>Humanity</i> Breaking Point for a vampire.<br /> <br /> Thinking on it further, why is the Vinculum so horrible? It comes down to the fact that it violates the victim's free will. It removes their ability to refuse the vampire because their will has been altered so that they <i>want</i> to do what ever the vampire says, despite the fact that they probably <i>wouldn't</i> want that were it not for the influence of the Vinculum. In effect, it deprives them of consent. That's actually covered on the sample Breaking Points, at Humanity 8 - it may not be a Discipline <i>per se</i>, but it is a power of the blood, and I think it should absolutely be included under the umbrella of &quot;depriving another of consent with a Discipline.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 04-20-2015, 11:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T05:24:44-07:00","id":487195,"title":"","content":"Funnily enough, just this topic (as described in the OP) is soon going to be a thing as a player character recently made a husband and father a ghoul.<br /> <br /> On-topic though: While I can see where Charlaquin is coming from by placing it at Humanity 8, I would place it one step lower. Disciplines are usually temporary and immediate, a Vinculum is a more permanent thing and a more constant reminder of what you have done. That being said, I feel this is one of the Breaking Points where it really depends on the circumstances. Perversion, for instance, would likely be much higher than that, as would instances where the Vinculum is not used (intentionally anyway) to create a slave but out of some misguided sense of affection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T10:41:05-07:00","id":487431,"title":"","content":"It seems like, by default, one might consider Majesty as Humanity 9 and Dominate as Humanity 8. I would place Vinculum as worse than both. One, it's (practically, if not literally) permanent rather than temporary. Two, and this is more subjective, it's a more visceral sort of monstrous behavior. There is no real physical feedback regarding your monstrosity when using a discipline. Vinculum involves bleeding yourself and having someone else imbibe it. Humans don't do that. It's inhuman, so probably should count as a sin.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, it's heavily implied that most vampires of any standing at all will have some measure of bound human servants to act as eyes and ears and hands and feet to maintain their standing. If it were a terribly damning experience, this couldn't be the norm without vampire society being much more dominated by the Beast than it is already.<br /> <br /> So while my personal stance on mind control would put it as something only an Ancient bordering on Draugr would do, it just isn't reasonable. From a fluff/mechanics/playability standpoint, I'd probably have to put it at Humanity 7 at worst. The drop from 7 to 6 kind of symbolizes a neonate's dive into actual vampire life, as evidenced by being both the first drop for a new character and the one that activates the Clan Bane, and it seems like vinculum is a core part of actual vampire life. Placing it lower would seem counter-thematic to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1692,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","user":"Maina","date":". 04-21-2015, 01:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T10:51:46-07:00","id":487433,"title":"","content":"I just don't understand ranking Vinculum lower than creating a ghoul, since the process of creating a ghoul inherently includes all the blood bonding of a Vinculum and <i>also</i> turns the person into a ghoul. Why is it worse if you just bond them but don't spend the willpower to ghoul them?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T11:06:28-07:00","id":487465,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point?p=487433#post487433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just don't understand ranking Vinculum lower than creating a ghoul, since the process of creating a ghoul inherently includes all the blood bonding of a Vinculum and <i>also</i> turns the person into a ghoul. Why is it worse if you just bond them but don't spend the willpower to ghoul them?</div> </div> </div> </div>Two possibilities, neither of which I'm saying is objectively true:<br /> <br /> 1. Writer oversight. Doesn't make a ton of sense to me putting Ghouls and using Disciplines to remove consent on the same level, but there we go.<br /> 2. (More likely?) Ghouling actually gives something back. The Vinculum is incidental. You're treating the (ex-)mortal closer to an equal than the entirely one-sided Vinculum. You're giving them superpowers and (conditional) immortality in exchange for helping you out. It's more like an arrangement between sapient beings than the one-sided enslavement of vanilla Vinculum. Of course, depends on if you're doing it with their consent or not, but breaking points are explicitly meant to be fluid guidelines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T12:45:44-07:00","id":487518,"title":"","content":"I think maybe the reason it's not a breaking point is that it's not really overtly supernatural. You give someone a bit of your blood to drink, they <i>really</i> like it and after a few times they suddenly really <i>really</i> like you.<br /> <br /> On the visceral level humanity works at, the human side of their mind just... doesn't get it. And the Beast doesn't really get the appeal. So neither half really knows what to make of it. The closest equivalent would probably be slipping someone a roofie, but if you notice that's not on there either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T16:57:21-07:00","id":487799,"title":"","content":"While uncommon, you can also ghoul a character without giving them a full vinculum (i.e. so they can heal a wound). It's ... improbable that they would place it there for just such a specific incident, though, but it is a good reason for those of us who put it below 8 to use. <br /> <br /> Re-reading the list, I wouldn't place it at Humanity 9 because the full vinculum doesn't so much as urge as re-shape it in my mind. Also depriving a human of consent is a sin at 8 and the vinculum very much does that. Even if they consent to the bond, once the full vinculum sets in, they'll pretty much &quot;consent&quot; to anything if it's put to them right by the regnant and that puts the very idea of them being able to consent into question.<br /> <br /> Of course, part of our decisions on where to rank it will also likely connect with just how &quot;far gone&quot; we run those under full vinculum. If it just means that they want to please the person, sure, it's no worse than Confidante, but if we run it like Idol where otherwise well-attached and compassionate parents would suddenly and gladly kill their own children or offer them for feeding just to make the vampire happy, and even thank the vampire for the privilege of killing their children, then yeah, that's going to be as clearly vampiric as getting hit by a car because it's not so much &quot;urging&quot; as a &quot;personality rewrite to suit the vampire&quot;. It's a pretty heavy enforcement of will in the latter case and there's no human equivalent -- or even equivalent among the other monster types.<br /> <br /> The other option would be to sit it at 8 (or 7 in line with it being a &quot;Welcome to Vampiricism&quot; sin), and then just hit the PC with breaking points if the person suffering from the vinculum does something terrible in the vampire's name that they wouldn't ordinarily do because of the blood bond*. This would probably be the most interesting option for me as it allows for situations where the vinculum (i.e. with ghouling) plus ghouldom seems to me like something that could disturb a very new vampire. So after all this conversation, I'll probably lift it to 7. Welcome to being a Vampire. Your blood is a drug that enslaves humanity. Once you hit Humanity 6, you won't look twice at it.<br /> <br /> *NOTE: This isn't to say that a GM should lay in wait to spring such a trap, only that it might make good story if the PCs get lazy in their choice of who to vinculum, such as using to barrel through humans without any real further interaction to back it up and smooth it down. It makes me think of the injured girl from Vampire: the Masquerade Bloodlines who eventually traps a man in the closet for you. Also if this type of reaction is likely, some kind of Empathy roll to give the vampire fair warning and an OOC talk about just how hard core blood bonds are in your game are in order.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My horror roleplaying blog with a strong focus on World of Darkness and Call of Cthulhu can be found here: <a href=\"http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/\" target=\"_blank\">http://stwildonroleplaying.blogspot.com.au/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1248-laraqua","name":"Laraqua","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"107"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T19:55:19-07:00","id":487902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laraqua</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point?p=487799#post487799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While uncommon, you can also ghoul a character without giving them a full vinculum (i.e. so they can heal a wound). It's ... improbable that they would place it there for just such a specific incident, though, but it is a good reason for those of us who put it below 8 to use.<br /> <br /> Re-reading the list, I wouldn't place it at Humanity 9 because the full vinculum doesn't so much as urge as re-shape it in my mind. Also depriving a human of consent is a sin at 8 and the vinculum very much does that. Even if they consent to the bond, once the full vinculum sets in, they'll pretty much &quot;consent&quot; to anything if it's put to them right by the regnant and that puts the very idea of them being able to consent into question.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, when I remembered that was a Humanity 8 Breaking Point, I realized that's where I'd want vinculum to sit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laraqua</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point?p=487799#post487799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, part of our decisions on where to rank it will also likely connect with just how &quot;far gone&quot; we run those under full vinculum. If it just means that they want to please the person, sure, it's no worse than Confidante, but if we run it like Idol where otherwise well-attached and compassionate parents would suddenly and gladly kill their own children or offer them for feeding just to make the vampire happy, and even thank the vampire for the privilege of killing their children, then yeah, that's going to be as clearly vampiric as getting hit by a car because it's not so much &quot;urging&quot; as a &quot;personality rewrite to suit the vampire&quot;. It's a pretty heavy enforcement of will in the latter case and there's no human equivalent -- or even equivalent among the other monster types.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the effects of a stage 3 blood bond are +3 to social pools and Discipline rolls used on the Thrall, a perfect impression for all social maneuvering, an increase in Discipline durations, and directly harming the vampire becomes an Integrity Breaking Point at a -5 penalty. So I'd say it's closer to the former - the vampire actually still has to convince the thrall to do her bidding, either through Discipline use or mundane persuasion. The thrall is extremely suggestible and eager to please, but is <i>technically </i>still capable of refusing the vampire if she botches her roll. I'd still definitely call that depriving them of consent, but more on the level of slipping them a roofie than the mental reprogramming of Dominate or the total emotional control of Majesty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Laraqua</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/486919-vinculum-as-a-breaking-point?p=487799#post487799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other option would be to sit it at 8 (or 7 in line with it being a &quot;Welcome to Vampiricism&quot; sin), and then just hit the PC with breaking points if the person suffering from the vinculum does something terrible in the vampire's name that they wouldn't ordinarily do because of the blood bond*. This would probably be the most interesting option for me as it allows for situations where the vinculum (i.e. with ghouling) plus ghouldom seems to me like something that could disturb a very new vampire. So after all this conversation, I'll probably lift it to 7. Welcome to being a Vampire. Your blood is a drug that enslaves humanity. Once you hit Humanity 6, you won't look twice at it.<br /> <br /> *NOTE: This isn't to say that a GM should lay in wait to spring such a trap, only that it might make good story if the PCs get lazy in their choice of who to vinculum, such as using to barrel through humans without any real further interaction to back it up and smooth it down. It makes me think of the injured girl from Vampire: the Masquerade Bloodlines who eventually traps a man in the closet for you. Also if this type of reaction is likely, some kind of Empathy roll to give the vampire fair warning and an OOC talk about just how hard core blood bonds are in your game are in order.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I believe it was the devs' intention that forcing another person into an act that would constitute a Breaking Point for them is also a Breaking Point for you. I think I remember David H. mentioning that in a discussion of Majesty, anyway. That would be a good way to drive home the potential horrors of the Vinculum - even if you're not bothered by enslaving someone to your will by feeding them your blood <i>at first</i>, it might be a nasty shock when you actually <i>see</i> the crazy lengths they will go to just to please you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}